just think of it as a jaunty stroll in  the woods what could possibly go wrong  in the forest of the night i know this  is crazy late coming out i warned you  this was gonna happen though so don't  complain to me so this episode starts  with a really intriguing premise which  is basically that  a global forest every land mass on the  planet has grown this massive amount of  trees they're covering everything it's  happened on and nowhere it has happened  immediately nobody knows how nobody  knows why and that's just a great doctor  who kind of set up right there although  if i'm being honest this episode overall  is probably the weakest of series eight  so far now having said that it's still  really good i would not call it a bad  episode which just speaks to how strong  this series has been overall so far  there's a lot of good stuff that goes on  here the stuff that happens with clara  again it's just really solid her dynamic  with the doctor especially i just at  this point i really enjoy their  relationship i like the fact that she's  not traveling with him constantly and  rather he picks her up from time to time  i like that right now especially she  kind of has a better sense of him than  he even has of himself and with the way  that capaldi's playing the doctor i  actually really like that dynamic again  i keep going back to the same thing i  don't want her to stick around forever  but they're wrapping her up in a really  great way assuming that she is leaving  at the end of the series which is my  assumption that's the vibe i'm getting  the doctor himself  he's slightly off from me in this  episode not in the overall performance  it's fine but he's a little more at  least initially dismissive of children  than i think we've ever seen him one of  the things that was always kind of nice  especially with the matt smith version  was how he related better and easier to  children than he did to adults and i  really liked that aspect and i guess  this just kind of hammers home the fact  that this is not gonna be the same he's  not gonna have that thing that matt  smith had going which is fine it just it  happened to be one of my favorite  aspects of that character so it was kind  of a little sad for me to really get it  hammered home that that's not the case  anymore now that said he's not  completely dismissive of children but  the way he kind of brushes them off uh  in the early parts of this episode you  made me go a little bit no  whoa all right if you're gonna be grumpy  i guess you go all out danny pink more  solid work as we've been seeing there's  a lot of really good character work as  there has been across the whole series i  think it's the story itself is probably  the weakest thing while the initial  setup is intriguing what's actually done  with it is a little odd because you know  as i said it is global it's the entire  planet covered in this forest but we're  only dealing with this little sort of  section of london we deal with sort of  the microcosm of it which i guess can  work but it it puts things a little out  of proportion because all we're dealing  with is like this little group of kids  on a school trip and the doctor yeah  we're trying to deal with these global  implications as well it's a little out  of balance in that sense and then it  goes for a lot of very obvious things  like the fact that one of the kids gets  lost in is in a red-hooded overcoat  thing and yes there are wolves so hey  little red white are you a big bad wolf  it's it's a little too on the nose and  it does come back to some of the notions  that the doctor gives towards the end of  the episode so they they're not  completely out of place thematically but  i it's just such the obvious way to go i  kind of wish they hadn't and then the  actual resolution and the way it it pans  out i kind of like the explanation for  what it is that happens but the way that  it actually pans out i i don't know what  it was about it just came off to me as a  bit cheesy a little bit too cut and dry  ah no problem actually kill the moon had  the same issue with the resolution so  this isn't the first time the series  that an episode has sort of not quite  stuck the landing a little bit i do  think kill the moon was slightly more  successful overall as an episode than  this one is but they're kind of a double  act in terms of themes and the way the  story plays out with similar strengths  similar weaknesses we've got part one of  our two part finale airing tonight which  means we're finally gonna get a better  sense of whoever the heck missy is we  may not find out exactly but we're  definitely gonna spend more time with  her do you have a theory as to who she  is do you think she is some version of  someone we've seen before or do you  think she's an altogether new character  whatever it is that you think drop  something down in the comments let's  talk about it until next time this  council is adjourned
 

